  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "The fire inspector is inspecting Claude Monet. The kitchen is in chaos. Let's listen closely to their exchange in Russian first, and then walk through each line together.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, может помочь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Меня он оштрафовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Тебя депортировать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, что это?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "С другим может...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Договориться удастся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хотя...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Если ты ему жизнь спасёшь...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, он нас и простит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Стульчик, да?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's review the scene line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Шеф, может помочь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, can I help?' There is no question word here — in Russian, rising intonation on 'to help' is enough to make it a question. And Chef jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't know.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'know' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Меня он оштрафовал.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He fined me.' Notice how 'me' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'to fine'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Тебя депортировать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You must be deported.' Chef Viktor links 'you' directly with 'deport' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, что это?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I'm thinking, what is this?' Chef Viktor combines 'I think' with 'this' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Думаю, нет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I don't think so.' Chef Viktor combines 'I think' with 'no' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "С другим может...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe with another...' Chef Viktor links 'with' directly with 'other' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Договориться удастся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We could negotiate.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'to agree' with 'to succeed'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There.' Ainura answers concisely with the word for 'there'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хотя...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Though...' Ainura answers concisely with the word for 'at least'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Если ты ему жизнь спасёшь...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'If you save his life...' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'if' with 'to save'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Может, он нас и простит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maybe he will forgive us.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'can' with 'will forgive'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Стульчик, да?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A chair, right?' Notice how 'little chair' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'yes'. Let's listen to the dialog one more time.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
